\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention rather than reshaping visual geometry: training effects concentrate at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$, attention $+46$pp) rather than images ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).
This explains Grant et al.'s~\cite{grant2026} visual dominance finding: DINOv2 has the highest geometric alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$).
Seven analyses support distributed L1 attention redistribution: \emph{interventional} (dose-response, $d = 0.48 \to 0.64$; counterfactual, $d = 0.63$--$0.97$), \emph{supporting} (attention gradient $0 \to 9 \to 24$ heads; $R^2$ ablation $+0.059$; trajectory $d = 0.65$), and \emph{constraining} (bootstrap CIs, $p = 0.0018$; specificity control: trajectory-$d$ ratio $1.08\times$; $R^2$ $z = -3.11$, $0$\textsuperscript{th} percentile).
Extended mechanistic interpretation and functional stage analysis are in Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic_interpretation}--\ref{sec:functional_stages}.

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}
We study OpenVLA-7B on held-out LIBERO; attention patterns may differ across scales (Appendix~\ref{sec:ood_diagnostics},~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}).
Both architectures use causal-decoder designs; flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} VLAs use cross-attention, so our findings may not generalize to non-autoregressive decoders.
Llama-2's 9/32 pretrained instruction heads may reflect text bias ($+15$-head delta isolates fine-tuning); all analyses are open-loop, complementing H\"aon et al.'s~\cite{haon2025} (\emph{what}) analysis.

